---
title: "Publish from SQS"
description: "Configure Outpost to ingest published events from an AWS SQS queue."
---

This guide provides information on using SQS to publish events to Outpost.

## Message Structure

SQS messages should have the same payload structure as the [Publish API endpoint](/docs/outpost/publishing/events).

```json
{
  "tenant_id": "<TENANT_ID>",
  "destination_id": "<DESTINATION_ID>", // Optional. Provide a way of routing events to a specific destination
  "topic": "topic.name", // Topic defined in TOPICS environment variable
  "eligible_for_retry": true | false, // Should event delivery be retried? Default is true.
  "metadata": Payload, // can be any JSON payload,
  "data": Payload // can be any JSON payload
}
```

## Failed messages

When Outpost fails to process a message, it makes the message visible again and SQS redelivers it immediately, with no backoff and no limit. This includes messages that can never succeed, such as invalid JSON, `data` that is not a JSON object, or a missing `topic` when `TOPICS` is set. A message larger than `MAX_EVENT_SIZE_BYTES` is the exception: Outpost deletes it on its first delivery, so it never reaches your dead-letter queue.

Dead-lettering and redelivery limits are configured on your queue, not in Outpost. Set a [redrive policy](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html) with a dead-letter queue and `maxReceiveCount`. Outpost resets the visibility timeout on failure, so the queue's visibility timeout does not delay redeliveries.

`PUBLISH_MAX_REDELIVERIES` sets a limit on the Outpost side instead: once a message has been redelivered that many times, Outpost deletes it. `0` deletes a message on its first failure. To keep those messages, set `maxReceiveCount` no higher than `PUBLISH_MAX_REDELIVERIES` so your dead-letter queue receives them first.

## Configuration

Provide Outpost with connection and routing information for your SQS instance used for publishing events.

### Environment Variables

```
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_REGION="<REGION>"
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_QUEUE="<QUEUE_NAME>"
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_ACCESS_KEY_ID="<KEY_ID>"
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="<SECRET>"
```

Optionally, you can provide the `PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_ENDPOINT` environment variable to specify the endpoint URL for the SQS service. This can be useful for local development or when using a non-standard SQS endpoint.

#### Example

```
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_REGION="eu-north-1"
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_QUEUE="outpost-pub-queue"
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_ACCESS_KEY_ID="REDACTED"
PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="REDACTED"
```

### YAML

```yaml
publishmq:
  aws_sqs:
    region: <AWS_REGION>
    queue: <QUEUE_NAME>
    access_key_id: <KEY_ID>
    secret_access_key: <SECRET>

    # Optional.
    # Useful for local development or when using a non-standard SQS endpoint.
    endpoint: <URL>

    # Optional. See "Assuming an IAM role".
    role_arn: <ROLE_ARN>
    external_id: <EXTERNAL_ID>
    role_session_name: <SESSION_NAME>
    sts_region: <AWS_REGION>
```

#### Example

```yaml
publishmq:
  aws_sqs:
    region: eu-north-1
    queue: outpost-pub-queue
    access_key_id: REDACTED
    secret_access_key: REDACTED
```

### Assuming an IAM role

Outpost can read the queue through an IAM role. It assumes the role with the access key, or with the AWS SDK default credential chain (environment variables, shared config, or the role of the instance, task or pod it runs on) when no key is set.

1. Create an IAM role with a trust policy that names the identity Outpost runs as. The `Condition` is optional; include it to require an external ID.

   ```json
   {
     "Version": "2012-10-17",
     "Statement": [
       {
         "Effect": "Allow",
         "Principal": { "AWS": "<ARN of the user or role Outpost runs as>" },
         "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
         "Condition": { "StringEquals": { "sts:ExternalId": "<EXTERNAL_ID>" } }
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

2. Give the role the [required permissions](#required-permissions) on the queue:

   ```json
   {
     "Version": "2012-10-17",
     "Statement": [
       {
         "Effect": "Allow",
         "Action": [
           "sqs:ChangeMessageVisibility",
           "sqs:DeleteMessage",
           "sqs:GetQueueUrl",
           "sqs:ReceiveMessage"
         ],
         "Resource": "arn:aws:sqs:<REGION>:<ACCOUNT_ID>:<QUEUE_NAME>"
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

3. If Outpost runs in a different AWS account from the role, also allow its identity to call `sts:AssumeRole` on the role.
4. Configure Outpost with the role. Leave out the access key to use the default credential chain. Set the external ID only if the trust policy requires one.

   ```
   PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_REGION="<REGION>"
   PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_QUEUE="<QUEUE_NAME>"
   PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_ROLE_ARN="arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:role/<ROLE_NAME>"
   PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_EXTERNAL_ID="<EXTERNAL_ID>"
   ```

The session is named `outpost` in the role account's CloudTrail logs; set `PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_ROLE_SESSION_NAME` to change it.

Outpost calls STS in the queue's Region. If that Region isn't enabled for the identity Outpost runs as, such as an [opt-in Region](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/manage-acct-regions.html) enabled only in the queue's account, set `PUBLISH_AWS_SQS_STS_REGION` to a Region it can use, such as `us-east-1`.

### Required Permissions

The following permissions are required for the provided access key. When a role is set, they go on the role instead.

- `sqs:ChangeMessageVisibility`
- `sqs:DeleteMessage`
- `sqs:GetQueueUrl`
- `sqs:ReceiveMessage`

### Health

In a self-hosted deployment, if the publish consumer fails (it can't subscribe, or 10 receives fail in a row, about 70 seconds), `/healthz` reports it `failed` (HTTP 503) until the process restarts. You can turn on restarts instead by adding `publishmq` to `SUPERVISOR_RESTART_WORKERS`: Outpost then restarts the consumer with a backoff and reports it `degraded` while it retries. See [Supervisor](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#supervisor) for the limits after which it reports `failed`.

